From a970a7eb1049475bfb6feb2aa0b15b1e95a4d452 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Wayland: give a Vulkan window two views while an engine draws
 a pair

An engine that draws a window's elements once per eye, as the edition's
Qt Quick does for a style that shows depth, asks the platform for two
views of the window's surface with the window's _q_stereo_views. Only
the Wayland EGL window honoured the request, so with the Vulkan RHI
such a window was never drawn as a pair, on any driver, although a
swapchain with two image layers presents there.

The Wayland Vulkan window now takes the request too. Whoever makes the
window's swapchain asks the platform how many views it should have (the
window's "_q_stereo_views" native resource) and says how many it got
("_q_stereo_surface_views"). The window then reports a stereo format
and marks the pair, with kde_stereo_depth_v1, as the desktop's own
depth, while the Vulkan implementation declares the two layers as a
side-by-side pair. A window that asked for stereo buffers itself keeps
its two views; only the mark follows the engine.

The Vulkan swapchain took its layers once, from the format the window
had when it was built. It now follows the wanted number at the start of
a frame that acquires an image, never between the parts of a frame that
an engine renders an eye at a time. Building a swapchain again with
another number of views released the image resources of the old one by
the new count; they are released by their own.

With one view again the platform also makes the surface again: the
declaration of a pair belongs to the surface, and the Vulkan stereo
layer keeps it for as long as the surface lives, so the one picture was
shown as the two halves of a pair on the drivers that layer serves.

Where the surface offers one image layer the window is shown as one
picture, and a warning says why, once per window.

QVulkanWindow builds a swapchain of its own and has no way to draw an
eye; it is unchanged.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
